// ======== Layer 3, stages between actors: chunks that move under credit ========
//
// Between two actors a stream is a run of owned chunks (an IoBuf each), handed
// on by a call and given back by another. Two actor traits say what a stage
// is to its neighbours; neither names the other's actor, so any actor that has
// the behaviours is a stage (the manual's Actor traits).
//
//   ChunkSink    what takes chunks:  accept(c)   a chunk, moved in
//                                    end(how)    the stream is over: Ending
//                                    attach(up)  who to give buffers back to
//   ChunkSource  what gets them back: giveBack(b) an emptied buffer
//
// The end, and an error, travel FORWARD as the call 'end(how)', after the last
// chunk and from the same sender, so they arrive in order. They are never a
// cancel. An 'Ending' is Finished or Failed(AnyError): the AnyError is Sendable,
// so it crosses between actors, and a transform's 'failure()' makes it from the
// codec's own typed error (DeflateError, ZstdError) with nothing lost: its
// message, its chain of causes, and 'holds[E]()' to take the typed error out.
//
// THE CREDIT IS A WINDOW OF BYTES, THE MEMORY A POOL (chunkwindow.cone, chunkpool.cone). A
// stage owns a ChunkWindow, its stream's window (256 KiB by default), and sends only
// while the chunks it has out stay within it; the buffers come from a ChunkPool, shared
// by the stages of a pipeline or the streams of a connection and bounded by a cap,
// taken only when there is data and sized to it. The consumer gives the same
// buffer back when it has FINISHED with the chunk: 'giveBack' IS the
// acknowledgement "chunk n is done". A stage made without a pool has one of its
// own. A pipeline is wired from the sink end back: each stage
// is made with the stage after it, and tells that stage who it is with
// 'attach(selfactor)' in its 'init', so the consumer can give buffers back.
// The handles that point upstream are dropped at the end of the stream (when
// the stage has seen the upstream 'end' and returned every buffer it holds), so
// the actors do not keep each other alive.
//
// THE ORDER OF A STREAM (chunkinbox.cone). A stage that never awaits handles one
// chunk after another by the nature of an actor, and needs nothing more. A sink
// whose 'accept' awaits (a file write) is parked there while the actor runs its
// next message, which could be chunk n+1 or the 'end'; its stream keeps order with
// a ChunkInbox, the receiving half of the link, so that one stream's chunks are handled
// in the order sent and its 'end' after the last of them, and so that a stalled
// stream holds up no other. The promises each side makes, for a stream:
//
//   P1  the producer sends the chunks in order, each only while its window and the pool have room;
//   P2  it sends exactly one 'end' per stream, after its last chunk (Finished, or Failed(err)), and
//       nothing after it. It need not wait for the buffers to come back first: 'end' queues behind
//       the chunks at the sink;
//   S1  the sink handles one stream's chunks in the order sent, chunk n+1 starting only once n is
//       finished (its effect done, a write completed, say);
//   S2  it gives each buffer back only once finished with it;
//   S3  it handles 'end' only after every chunk before it, then passes it on or settles its result
//       (DigestSink's digest);
//   S4  it keeps each stream's queue and credit apart, so a slow or stalled stream never holds up
//       another sharing the stage, a connection or a pool.
//
// FAILURE. A sink that fails on chunk n gives back n and every queued buffer and
// sends 'end(Failed(err))' on; chunks that still arrive are given back unhandled.
// A producer that fails sends 'end(Failed(err))' in order after what it already
// sent; the sink handles those chunks, then fails. ABANDONMENT: a stage that quits
// stops sending and gives back what it holds; a ChunkInbox that is dropped frees what it
// queued; a ChunkWindow that is dropped returns its share of the pool.
//
// CANCELLATION. A stage works in the cancel group of the message it handles: a
// chunk and an 'end' come in the group the sender works in (the source's), a buffer
// given back in the consumer's. So a pipeline is cancelled from the consumer back
// by building it so: the consumer has a group Gc, the source runs in a child of Gc
// (actors.withGroup(&gc.child(), ...) around its 'start'), and the consumer quits
// by cancelling Gc, from a behaviour or from outside. The next stage up sees a
// cancelled group on the next buffer given back (or chunk arriving) and stops: it
// ends downstream with Failed (once; the hash stage also answers its digest sink
// with an error), gives its input back, and drops its upstream handle, so the
// actors do not keep each other alive even if the source never sends an 'end'. The
// stage above that sees the chunks it gave back, in the same group, and does the
// same, to the source. Buffers granted in flight still come back to their links;
// a chunk that arrives after the stage has stopped is freed. A stage in no group
// never sees a cancel.
//
//   // sink end first; each stage is made with its downstream
//   let count = Counter();
//   let hash  = HashStage[cryptohash.Sha256](count, cryptohash.Sha256.make(), whoGetsTheDigest);
//   let inf   = TransformStage[deflate.Inflater](hash, deflate.Inflater.make(deflate.Format.Gzip));
//   source.start(inf);
//
// TWO ACTORS, each generic. 'TransformStage[T]' holds any Transform with its ChunkWindow and
// does the work of a stage that makes new chunks (inflate, deflate, zstd): feed
// what arrives, pull what comes out into the link's buffers, and hold the input
// chunk until all its output is out. A pass-through such as a hash stage is
// 'HashStage[D]' over a Digester: the chunk goes on as it is, unmoved and
// uncopied, and comes back through the stage to the source. (Before generic
// actors each codec was an actor of its own over a 'StageCore[T]' or a
// 'HashCore[D]' that did the work; the actor is now the work.)
//
// 'sink <- chunk' is 'sink.accept(chunk)': the behaviour declares '<-' as its overload name.

// How a stream ended
pub enum Ending {
  // Every chunk has been sent
  Finished;
  // The stream could not be completed; the error says why
  Failed(AnyError);
}

// A stage that takes chunks: the chunk is moved in, the end follows the last
// chunk, and 'attach' comes before the first.
//
// WHO HOLDS WHOM. A sink may keep the 'up' it was attached with: that costs
// nothing, because whoever sends a sink its 'end' lets go of the sink in the
// same step (a stage sets its 'down' to None, a forwarder its target), so the
// source no longer holds the sink and the pair is not a reference cycle. A
// sender therefore sends nothing after 'end' and keeps no handle to the sink
// past it. A sink that keeps 'up' need not drop it on 'end'.
pub actor trait ChunkSink {
  // A chunk of the stream. Give it back to the source when its bytes are used.
  pub async fn accept overload `<-`(self, c: IoBuf);
  // The stream is over, one way or the other. Nothing is sent after.
  pub async fn end(self, how: Ending);
  // The stage that sends here, to give buffers back to; sent once, before the
  // first chunk.
  //
  // WHY 'attach' CANNOT BE OVERTAKEN by the first 'accept'. A source sends
  // 'attach' in its 'init', and 'accept' only from a behaviour that runs after
  // the init returned (a 'start' message, or a 'giveBack'), so the 'accept' is
  // caused by a send that came after the 'attach' in the creating thread. The
  // mailbox gives the consumer its messages in the order the sends claimed their
  // positions: a send claims with one sequentially consistent atomic add on the
  // mailbox's tail ('claim', packages/actors/src/actors.cone, THE MAILBOX) before
  // it returns, the consumer takes positions in order, and a message is never
  // overtaken by one it caused (the package header, MESSAGES). That holds across
  // the ring's segments and for senders on different workers
  // (actors/tests/ringfanin.cone checks each sender's order under fan-in). The
  // downstream stage therefore always knows its source before its first chunk.
  pub async fn attach(self, up: ChunkSource);
}

// A stage that lends buffers and takes them back
pub actor trait ChunkSource {
  // An emptied buffer back from its consumer: one more chunk may be sent
  pub async fn giveBack(self, b: IoBuf);
}

// Whoever wants the digest a hash stage worked out, 'Out' being the digest's
// type (Array[u8, 32] for sha256). It gets exactly one answer, when the stream
// ends: Ok with the digest if the stream finished, Err with the failure if it
// did not (the error that ended it, as text: see HashStage.end). So a waiter is
// never left waiting for a digest that will not come.
pub actor trait DigestSink[Out] {
  pub async fn digest(self, r: Result[Out, AnyError]);
}

// Gives a chunk back to its source, if the source is still held
fn handBack(up: &Option[ChunkSource], b: IoBuf) {
  match up {
    case let &Some(u) { u.giveBack(b); }
    case is None {}
  }
}

// ---- a stage that transforms: any Transform ----

// A stage over a transform 't', sending its output to 'down' in chunks of up to
// 'linkBytes', its stream's window 'linkWindow' bytes and a pool of its own. The
// transform comes in whole ('make' it first), and is the stage's from then on:
//
//   TransformStage[deflate.Inflater](down, deflate.Inflater.make(deflate.Format.Gzip))
//   TransformStage[deflate.Deflater](down, deflate.Deflater.make(deflate.Format.Gzip, 6i32))
//   TransformStage[zstd.Decompressor](down, zstd.Decompressor.make())
//   TransformStage[zstd.Compressor](down, zstd.Compressor.make(3i32))
//
// A stage of a pipeline or connection that shares a pool, and has a window of its
// own choosing, is made with the pool: TransformStage[T](down, t, pool, window).
pub actor TransformStage[T: Transform] is ChunkSink, ChunkSource {
  t: T;
  link: ChunkWindow;                 // the output's window, and its buffers from a pool
  down: Option[ChunkSink];    // the consumer; let go when our 'end' is sent
  up: Option[ChunkSource];
  cur: Option[IoBuf];         // the input chunk being worked on; held until its output is all out
  backlog: Deque[IoBuf];      // chunks that arrived while 'cur' was held (at most the upstream's window)
  waiting: bool;              // a pull wants an output buffer and the window or the pool is full
  finishing: bool;            // the transform has been told its input is over
  dry: bool;                  // a pull after that said NeedInput once
  upEnded: bool;              // the upstream's 'end' has arrived
  ended: bool;                // our 'end' has been sent downstream

  pub fn own overload init(self: &new, down: ChunkSink, t: T) {
    *self = Self(t: t, link: ChunkWindow.own(linkWindow), down: Some[ChunkSink](down), up: None[ChunkSource],
      cur: None[IoBuf], backlog: Deque[IoBuf](), waiting: false, finishing: false, dry: false,
      upEnded: false, ended: false);
    down.attach(selfactor);
  }

  // The same, its output buffers from a shared 'pool' and its window 'window' bytes
  pub fn shared overload init(self: &new, down: ChunkSink, t: T, pool: ChunkPool, window: usize) {
    *self = Self(t: t, link: ChunkWindow.make(window, pool), down: Some[ChunkSink](down), up: None[ChunkSource],
      cur: None[IoBuf], backlog: Deque[IoBuf](), waiting: false, finishing: false, dry: false,
      upEnded: false, ended: false);
    down.attach(selfactor);
  }

  // The stage that sends here (the sink's 'attach')
  pub async fn attach(self, source: ChunkSource) {
    up = Some[ChunkSource](source);
  }

  // A chunk arrives (the sink's 'accept')
  pub async fn accept(self, c: IoBuf) {
    self.cancelCheck();
    if ended {
      handBack(&up, c);
    } else if cur is Some || upEnded {
      backlog.push(c);
    } else {
      t.feed(c.view());
      cur = Some[IoBuf](c);
      self.drain();
    }
    self.settle();
  }

  // The upstream's end arrives (the sink's 'end')
  pub async fn end(self, how: Ending) {
    self.cancelCheck();
    upEnded = true;
    match how {
      case is Finished {
        if !waiting && !ended { self.drain(); }
      }
      case let Failed(e) {
        if !ended { self.finish(Ending.Failed(e)); }
      }
    }
    self.settle();
  }

  // An output buffer comes back (the source's 'giveBack')
  pub async fn giveBack(self, b: IoBuf) {
    link.give(b);
    self.cancelCheck();
    if waiting { self.drain(); }
    self.settle();
  }

  // CANCELLATION. The stage works in the cancel group of the message it handles
  // (a chunk and an end come in the group the sender works in; a buffer given back,
  // in the consumer's): when that group is cancelled, the stage stops. It sends
  // 'end(Failed)' on (once), pulls no more, and gives the input it holds back to
  // its upstream, which hears that its consumer has quit by the same cancelled
  // group on the buffers it gets back. Output buffers still granted downstream
  // return to the link as they come back. The upstream handle is dropped, as at any
  // end, when the upstream's own 'end' has come (settle). A pipeline built so that
  // each stage's group is a child of its consumer's therefore stops from the
  // consumer back to the source.
  fn cancelCheck(self: &mut) {
    if ended || !actors.isCancelled() { return; }
    self.finish(Ending.Failed(AnyError.msg("streams: cancelled")));
    self.flush();
    // The upstream is told by the chunks given back; its 'end' may never come (a
    // producer that does not look at its group), so the handle goes now. A chunk
    // that still arrives has nowhere to go back to and is freed.
    up = None[ChunkSource];
  }

  // Every input chunk held goes back to the upstream
  fn flush(self: &mut) {
    self.returnCur();
    var more = true;
    while more {
      match backlog.popFront() {
        case let Some(c) { handBack(&up, c); }
        case is None { more = false; }
      }
    }
  }

  // Pull while there is a buffer to pull into and something to pull
  fn drain(self: &mut) {
    waiting = false;
    self.cancelCheck();
    var go = !ended;
    while go {
      match link.take(linkBytes) {
        case is None {
          waiting = true;
          go = false;
        }
        case let Some(b) {
          var out = b;
          let st = t.pull(&mut out);
          if out.isEmpty() { link.give(out); } else { self.send(out); }
          if st == Status.Done {
            self.finish(Ending.Finished);
            go = false;
          } else if st == Status.Failed {
            self.finish(self.failed());
            go = false;
          } else if st == Status.NeedInput {
            go = self.nextInput();
          }
          // a long run of pulls stops at a cancel between chunks
          self.cancelCheck();
          if ended { go = false; }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // The input is used up: give the chunk back and go on to the next, or to the
  // end. Whether to pull again.
  fn nextInput(self: &mut) bool {
    self.returnCur();
    match backlog.popFront() {
      case let Some(c) {
        t.feed(c.view());
        cur = Some[IoBuf](c);
        return true;
      }
      case is None {}
    }
    if !upEnded { return false; }
    if !finishing {
      t.finish();
      finishing = true;
      return true;
    }
    if dry {
      self.finish(Ending.Failed(AnyError.msg("streams: the transform wants input after the stream's end")));
      return false;
    }
    dry = true;
    true
  }

  // Our end, once
  fn finish(self: &mut, how: Ending) {
    if ended { return; }
    ended = true;
    // the end is the last thing sent down, so the handle goes with it (the ChunkSink contract)
    var d = None[ChunkSink];
    d <-> down;
    match d {
      case let Some(k) { k.end(how); }
      case is None {}
    }
  }

  fn send(self: &mut, c: IoBuf) {
    match &down {
      case let &Some(k) { k.accept(c); }
      case is None { link.give(c); }   // 'c' is this stage's own output buffer, never upstream's
    }
  }

  fn failed(self: &) Ending {
    match t.failure() {
      case let Some(e) { Ending.Failed(e) }
      case is None { Ending.Failed(AnyError.msg("streams: the transform failed")) }
    }
  }

  fn returnCur(self: &mut) {
    var taken = None[IoBuf];
    taken <-> cur;
    match taken {
      case let Some(c) { handBack(&up, c); }
      case is None {}
    }
  }

  // Once both ends are over, every chunk held goes home and the upstream handle goes
  fn settle(self: &mut) {
    if ended && upEnded {
      self.returnCur();
      var more = true;
      while more {
        match backlog.popFront() {
          case let Some(c) { handBack(&up, c); }
          case is None { more = false; }
        }
      }
      up = None[ChunkSource];
    }
  }
}

// ---- a stage that passes chunks on and watches them: a digest ----

// A stage over a Digester 'd'. The chunk goes on as it is; the buffer comes
// back through this stage to the source. 'to' gets exactly one answer, when
// the stream ends, its digest type the digester's own ('D.Out'):
//
//   HashStage[cryptohash.Sha256](down, cryptohash.Sha256.make(), to)   // to: DigestSink[Array[u8, 32]]
pub actor HashStage[D: Digester] is ChunkSink, ChunkSource {
  d: D;
  down: Option[ChunkSink];    // let go when the end is sent (the ChunkSink contract)
  up: Option[ChunkSource];
  out: usize;                 // chunks passed on and not yet back
  upEnded: bool;
  to: DigestSink[D.Out];      // gets exactly one answer, when the stream ends
  stopped: bool;              // the stream's cancel group was cancelled: the answer and the end have gone

  pub fn init(self: &new, down: ChunkSink, d: D, to: DigestSink[D.Out]) {
    *self = Self(d: d, down: Some[ChunkSink](down), up: None[ChunkSource], out: 0usize, upEnded: false, to: to, stopped: false);
    down.attach(selfactor);
  }

  pub async fn attach(self, source: ChunkSource) {
    up = Some[ChunkSource](source);
  }

  // The stage works in the cancel group of the message it handles; when that
  // group is cancelled (TransformStage.cancelCheck) it answers the digest sink with an
  // error and ends downstream with Failed, once, and gives later chunks back
  fn cancelCheck(self: &mut) {
    if stopped || upEnded || !actors.isCancelled() { return; }
    stopped = true;
    to.digest(Err[D.Out, AnyError](AnyError.msg("streams: cancelled")));
    self.endDown(Ending.Failed(AnyError.msg("streams: cancelled")));
    up = None[ChunkSource];
  }

  // The one end downstream; the handle goes with it
  fn endDown(self: &mut, how: Ending) {
    var k = None[ChunkSink];
    k <-> down;
    match k {
      case let Some(s) { s.end(how); }
      case is None {}
    }
  }

  pub async fn accept(self, c: IoBuf) {
    self.cancelCheck();
    if upEnded || stopped {
      // nothing is sent after an 'end' (P2): given back unhandled
      handBack(&up, c);
      return;
    }
    d.feed(c.view());
    out = out + 1usize;
    match &down {
      case let &Some(k) { k.accept(c); }
      case is None { handBack(&up, c); }
    }
  }

  pub async fn giveBack(self, b: IoBuf) {
    self.cancelCheck();
    out = out - 1usize;
    handBack(&up, b);
    self.settle();
  }

  // Answers the digest sink, once: the digest if the stream finished, else an
  // error. An AnyError cannot be copied, so the sink gets the failure as text
  // (its report) and the stage after this one gets the original, typed.
  // Then passes the end on.
  pub async fn end(self, how: Ending) {
    self.cancelCheck();
    upEnded = true;
    if stopped {
      // already answered and ended on a cancel
      self.settle();
      return;
    }
    match &how {
      case is Finished { to.digest(Ok[D.Out, AnyError](d.finish())); }
      case let &Failed(e) {
        var text = String();
        {
          var w = text.textOut();
          e.report(&uni w);
        }
        to.digest(Err[D.Out, AnyError](AnyError.msg(text.view())));
      }
    }
    self.endDown(how);
    self.settle();
  }

  fn settle(self: &mut) {
    if upEnded && out == 0usize { up = None[ChunkSource]; }
  }
}
